#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228866 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eca32dd-1563-55c6-b594-08588efd601b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/id #uuid "3638fd11-5f61-5656-89c4-18ee0a4318ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-21720" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/cf38da5e2aec9fc3957f52abc58fc396686db632561953991a3ca0fc23799ace.png)\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/1a1e4510a1ea4961976e22504d6be845edffd972cc88dda0fde4608b50be2b09.png) where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/cfc60391cfb7369d109e7d56b89b6bfd1f97e7251aacb637f61c94d24cdb4f00.png)*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/802292289f84a7d69cfaadd91d88d6a4af9560f618dfae6b553fbca16b63cb96.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/a851d7868235cdaf8cd6914d21b648b3bd7453549748f4bacc3caf5485c17e50.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/696a10263a0fe8a52e0da4a413956bb4040220de2c22ae41f76032f7045a5265.png) So, the matrix of the system is ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/c62e0b111e20b925570d7f178ac556293597b071d9aefb821adc70d83afcb91e.png)\n\nGiven the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x(t_0)$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/b7b3cf84162533f6b86e3faf8bba17b335ade35acb42d18aa768a5a1db900730.png)\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t = t_0 = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x(0) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/5f80485e8a40e46b5c11656dae755f51f7b0617cbe8b4369cf330f329d042283.png)\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3s+3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3s+3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3s+3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/dbce2a7dd9077db8a6e4f85a0b54c9d2563cad3a0ef5a0a98606ca7a4bb5f81f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228867 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c4b78fa-76af-5261-87a0-d1960aa0fe04" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/id #uuid "c24150ed-748b-5069-9fc5-48d4e6deaef2" :question/math-academy-id "e-21721" :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system  $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $\\mathbf{x}(t)$ passing through $\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ is presented below.\n\n$\\textrm{L1}{:}\\;$ Let $\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L2}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t) = -Ae^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t) + e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}'(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L3}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}'(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(t)$  \n\n$\\textrm{L4}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)-\\mathbf{y}(t_0) = \\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\n$\\textrm{L5}{:}\\;$ $\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)$ \n\n$\\textrm{L6}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{-A(s-t_0)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\textrm{L7}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\n$\\textrm{L8}{:}\\;$ $e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{y}(t)=\\mathbf{x}(t)$\n\n$\\textrm{L9}{:}\\;$ $\\displaystyle\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)+\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)}\\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s$\n\nFill in the blanks with the correct reasons to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from $\\textrm{L2}$ by  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/befe715ac2d9375061b1a3c8c0528a0bfb2764197cee4eca4d2b60146cec20b6.png).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/09febb48bb00d22caebf088103181b5db6377befccad18f1370718351cc91e9d.png).\n\n$\\quad$ Line $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/999db06dcfe0878ddb3316b11546668ba9442d8fd110165e5076b90964b84905.png)." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\textrm{L2}$ and $\\textrm{L3}.$ Starting from\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}'(t) = -Ae^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t) + e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}'(t)\n$$\n\nin $\\textrm{L2}$ and substituting $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)$ from the original system gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}(Ax(t)+g(t)) \\\\  & =-A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+A{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t)+{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}g(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $\\textrm{L3}$ follows from $\\textrm{L2}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/9dcfd90e4444195ee781ab388b36a575ec1219d1820f7141114e29311941ddc5.png)\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1}$ and $\\textrm{L5}.$ Evaluating $\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)$ at $t=t_0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t_0)=e^{-A(t_0-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=I\\mathbf{x}(t_0)=\\mathbf{x}(t_0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L5}$ follows from line $\\textrm{L1}$  by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/90a07cffb30b2ef860b0d3314865a7bd9ca0b7c86811a63b07628c768188af3e.png)\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\textrm{L1}$ and $\\textrm{L8}.$ Multiplying both sides of\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{y}(t)=e^{-A(t-t_0)}\\mathbf{x}(t)\n$$\n\nfrom $\\textrm{L1}$ by $e^{A(t-t_0)}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}y(t) & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}{e}^{-A(t-{t}_{0})}x(t) \\\\  & =x(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\textrm{L8}$ follows from $\\textrm{L1}$ by ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/23a2ac9e75c9bac22187814f524d919166d3748f1caa5831d664231a80bac296.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228868 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/id #uuid "e151cd67-cf96-5378-9380-e9960c1eb6b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-21722" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/101a0c499e4eb8557917785388737fb12c85713d204efaad74b13720d53d90a9.png)\nFill in the blanks in the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/b1b5a17a8facd73638545861a9ec05ee2fdebdc2ae8f327fc86f1517085bcd77.png) where ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/52808e8499c76638f512feb7dd3721814844cbfb29deb7126ed9e8872a699031.png)*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\, \\\\ 3{e}^{4(t-1)}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given the system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\underbrace{e^{A(t-t_0)} \\, \\mathbf{x}(t_0)}_{\\text{homogeneous part}} + \\underbrace{\\int_{t_0}^{t} e^{A(t-s)} \\mathbf{g}(s) \\, \\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x(t_0)$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/ed2abaaded6d82b6ab9ae856b8f31f99d97bd34515715e36b71d2731049e5e75.png) So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{4(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-1)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3(t-1){e}^{4(t-1)} \\\\ 3{e}^{4(t-1)}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071250/assets/56f10da4c0759fd5616557c00ce5aa1062190b3fbaaa2ab0dff0d50ef3fb2615.png)\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3(t-1){e}^{4(t-1)} \\\\ 3{e}^{4(t-1)}\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eca32dd-1563-55c6-b594-08588efd601b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad39653-4f8f-5ccb-9769-cda7aa00b02d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370082} {:answer/id #uuid "351a60ca-4701-5d75-a1ce-f24582f45662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+3\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ad39653-4f8f-5ccb-9769-cda7aa00b02d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-3\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370082} :answer-field/id #uuid "8797cf8a-16d2-50d9-ae11-06f25b328ee8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f94a953-d6ae-5d71-b1d5-f509cb5bc306" :question/math-academy-id "q-334531" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+1,\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c4b78fa-76af-5261-87a0-d1960aa0fe04" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a7ffdc9-9f9a-5626-ae51-7fda526b5946" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228137} {:answer/id #uuid "9b60bd2e-e9f1-535f-b8c0-c0049ffbbcc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228138} {:answer/id #uuid "16710e53-2928-58a4-94a8-8448a9d69cc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228139} {:answer/id #uuid "37d5e975-eeee-5d0c-afab-73f4aa2b2413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b60bd2e-e9f1-535f-b8c0-c0049ffbbcc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228138} :answer-field/id #uuid "736163e7-5385-5bb0-8576-f6f56e55ea29" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228136} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2a0ab4-ccc5-5fdb-9e01-e60199005b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228142} {:answer/id #uuid "b38ef677-b641-5040-accc-571815bc6c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "substituting $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + g(t)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228143} {:answer/id #uuid "50b5a1a2-03f9-598b-ac48-ff8c671db004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "applying a change of variables" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228144} {:answer/id #uuid "54620513-6509-54fa-a510-d4cf8a794fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating and using the product rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54620513-6509-54fa-a510-d4cf8a794fec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating and using the product rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228145} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfaa1742-379c-5afa-977d-7f82f4923ba5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228141} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb0be037-1284-56f4-8039-381982c5fc96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating and using the sum rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228151} {:answer/id #uuid "3ad1ec6d-3a6c-5ca2-826b-f6ad4ed65aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by $e^{At}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228152} {:answer/id #uuid "57d07bb3-de1b-5893-883d-d48835635778" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "evaluating at $t = 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228153} {:answer/id #uuid "62e3a038-a1c0-5359-b4f7-a6e7b0f4fc12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "adding $e^{At}x(0)$ to both sides" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ad1ec6d-3a6c-5ca2-826b-f6ad4ed65aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "multiplying both sides by $e^{At}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228152} :answer-field/id #uuid "b897e708-3b14-534f-a6c6-daaa9bc2cad4" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228150} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8702f907-3bd2-5237-81f8-c01695050ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370085} {:answer/id #uuid "83a4e4ce-ebd7-5315-a33d-f4610afe7777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370086} {:answer/id #uuid "0ccf4b39-65d3-57e3-81bd-8bd2157b88a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ccf4b39-65d3-57e3-81bd-8bd2157b88a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{L3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370087} :answer-field/id #uuid "149e2923-8a99-59cc-a19a-30ed0039af2e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d5aa81c-82a3-50e6-9a6f-bc0b6662f32d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334533" :question/problem "Consider the inhomogeneous system  ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let $y(t)={e}^{-At}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-At}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $y(t)-y(0)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y(0)=x(0)$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $y(t)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line\n{{field-2}}\nby integrating both sides from $0$ to \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by  \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Differentiating $y(t)={e}^{-At}x(t)$ and applying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{-At})^{′}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-A){e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{differentiating and using the product rule}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Integrating ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-At}g(t)$ from $0$ to $t$ in $\\text{L3}$ yields\n\n$$\ny(t)-y(0)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by integrating both sides from $0$ to $t.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ Multiplying both sides of\n\n$$\ny(t)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,ds\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L6}$ by ${e}^{At}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}y(t) & ={e}^{At}x(0)+{e}^{At}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L6}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides by }{e}^{At}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef92c2d6-e23b-52bf-a008-51a201a9c5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef92c2d6-e23b-52bf-a008-51a201a9c5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370090} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b2dcad9-7d81-5273-ba9b-6800a7451abe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fde54945-356f-555a-9b82-4ec0afc8626d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fde54945-356f-555a-9b82-4ec0afc8626d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370092} :answer-field/id #uuid "70484772-838c-5ccc-b4ff-fe32577b7dbc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d810864-d336-576e-b35c-2f77a051b06d" :question/math-academy-id "q-334824" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | $(t-s){e}^{3t}$ |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35ed53d0-c307-5afc-8796-62c65e3a9b0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{5(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35ed53d0-c307-5afc-8796-62c65e3a9b0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{5(t-1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370095} :answer-field/id #uuid "32036138-b4c0-5251-953d-08c1537c6ea8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370094} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cd5b35e-88ec-5e28-8f15-1a1e200d4d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4cd5b35e-88ec-5e28-8f15-1a1e200d4d99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{5t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370097} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd155588-c6a7-53b7-9369-4678074ffc3f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4a7f2ab-105a-5f1d-9c97-2c5083b74394" :question/math-academy-id "q-334848" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{5(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5(t-1)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{5(t-1)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{5(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{5(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{5(t-1)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "423ae4ef-8d07-568b-b82a-70e2a1936338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2(t-s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "423ae4ef-8d07-568b-b82a-70e2a1936338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(2(t-s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370100} :answer-field/id #uuid "c58e9257-a9e1-5200-bc88-fe68179b6476" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370099} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cd0cc4a-6b26-5780-8e7f-b805cfb2d4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cd0cc4a-6b26-5780-8e7f-b805cfb2d4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cos(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370102} :answer-field/id #uuid "b64b9938-ae71-5cba-8774-1f811a0579e2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9799dccb-8c2e-5bac-a3cc-6ad14202208c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334855" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  | $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a93f5b88-c681-51cc-8d7e-02a027a58316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin(4(t-s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370105} {:answer/id #uuid "239cbcbd-98d6-59e6-8d4c-d96c59f384f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(4(t-s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370106}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "239cbcbd-98d6-59e6-8d4c-d96c59f384f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos(4(t-s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370106} :answer-field/id #uuid "3271e08a-e259-5eb8-8c98-ecb767e88996" :answer-field